WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.217
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan here.

00:00:07.217 --> 00:00:11.120
Welcome to our Daily Homeroom live stream.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.250
As I always explained,

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.410
this is a way for us to
stay together connected

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.020
in this time of school closures.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.440
Khan Academy, we're not for profit,

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:20.760
with a mission of providing a free

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.740
world-class education for anyone anywhere.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.110
And we could never foreseen the situation

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.860
that we're now founding ourselves in,

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.120
but over the last roughly decade,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.000
we've been building resources

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.910
that we think can really help you

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.490
as a student, a parent, a teacher,

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.880
keeps students learning
over this a very unfortunate

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.920
situation we are going through.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.050
I will remind everyone, before we get

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.410
into the mid of today's session,

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:46.670
we are not for profit,

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:48.820
we exist because of
philanthropic donations

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.120
from folks like yourself.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.500
We were running at a deficit,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.480
even before this whole Covid crisis hit,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.930
and now our traffic is almost three times

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.270
what it typically is.

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.960
I do wanna give a special thank you

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.610
for all of you who've donated over

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.390
the last several weeks,
and especially several

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:06.200
of these corporations you see here.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.489
Bank of America really
stepped up 31st weekend

00:01:08.489 --> 00:01:10.800
where they saw that we had a need,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.570
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Novartis.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.590
This is helping a lot,
but we need more help.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:19.050
We are continuing to run
at an increasing deficit.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:22.350
I also wanna give a special
thanks to my wife (murmurs)

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.710
this is my first Covid haircut (laughs)

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.560
I got over the weekend in our backyard.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.000
I think she's got some skills,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.480
obviously I'm biased.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.310
So today we're gonna have a fun session,

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.370
I'm gonna bring on two of my team members,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.840
Meaghan and Jeremy,
who are former teachers

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.650
who have been leading the Webinars

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.160
that we keep talking about,

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.910
over on these live streams.

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:48.110
And the collectively what we're gonna do,

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:50.530
on Friday we had a fun ask me anything,

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.550
where people were asking
literally anything,

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.360
And now I'm joined by Meaghan and Jeremy,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.250
and you can ask us anything.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.760
And obviously Meaghan
and Jeremy are expert at,

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.270
how does Khan Academy work in a classroom?

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.010
They're the ones running the webinars

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:04.900
and they can talk more about,

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.590
some of the webinars are about to run,

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.560
common questions they've seen,

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.290
but also ask us anything about anything.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.680
So Meaghan, Jeremy, thanks for joining us.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.150
Maybe a good place to start,

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:15.990
Meaghan, I'll start with you.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:16.823
Tell us a little bit about

00:02:16.823 --> 00:02:18.190
these webinars you're running

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.963
and what types of things
you're trying to get across.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.033
- Sure, thanks Sal.

00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:25.810
We are running webinars for teachers

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:28.100
who are transitioning from being

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.900
in the classroom everyday
with their students

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.000
to teaching and learning remotely.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.790
And we've covered everything from,

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.380
how to set up your Khan Academy account

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.360
to best practices, to even having teachers

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.310
who are really exceptional ambassadors,

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:45.540
who are sharing their best practices

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.660
for different students in different areas.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.240
So it could be math,

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.240
could be English language learners,

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.290
could be science.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.910
And so we're having both our team share

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.180
some of the things that
we know work really well,

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.960
along with teachers around the country

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.668
sharing their best ideas,

00:03:01.668 --> 00:03:04.440
to really form a community of teaching

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.640
and learning remotely.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.690
And we have two webinars
coming up this week.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.790
One for best practices
for supporting students

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.650
who are special education,

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.150
and one for best practices for supporting

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:17.420
English language learners.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.123
And those are on Wednesday
and Friday respectively.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:22.583
- That's super useful.

00:03:22.583 --> 00:03:24.000
And Jeremy, what would you say

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.000
that you and Meaghan are seeing as some

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.390
of the main, I guess pain points

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.040
that parents and teachers are facing,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.090
and what they are finding value in

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:34.343
from these webinars?

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:37.340
- It's a great question.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.530
And we definitely live
in webinar mode nowadays,

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:41.070
so thanks to everyone
who's been joining us over

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.830
the last couple of weeks, as we move

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.090
into this new sort of world order.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:45.923
That being said,

00:03:45.923 --> 00:03:46.820
I think what we're hearing, especially

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.090
from educators as well as parents,

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:51.660
is the hunger for specificity.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.500
We're now a month into this thing

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:54.860
and this seems to be the sort of the way

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:55.693
the world's gonna work,

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:57.350
at least for the next several weeks.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:58.880
How do we make the most of this time?

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.000
How do we serve our students

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.660
and our children the best way possible?

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.540
And so what we've been
hearing from teachers is,

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.250
how do I do that at the elementary level

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:08.670
versus the high school level?

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.320
How do I do that in my science class

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.060
versus my ELA class?

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.700
And so for the last several weeks,

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.470
we've been having ambassadors
share their specialty,

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.210
in each of those different areas

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:19.770
with educators across the country

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:21.080
and around the world.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.450
And next week is no different.

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:23.910
And we wanna make sure that,

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.840
whether you serve students
in special education,

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.350
or English language learners,

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:28.920
you're getting everything you need

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.770
to serve those students successfully.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.140
- And what advice...

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:35.190
Meaghan I ask you first,

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.260
what advice do you have
for teachers who are...

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.530
You know, they didn't get a lot of notice,

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:41.390
that they're now trying to figure out

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.070
how to transition their class

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.713
and teach virtually over
Skype, Zoom, whatever.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:51.033
Meaghan, what's your top tip or top tips?

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:53.500
- I think you're really hitting

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.270
the nail on the head Sals,

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.740
that we're seeing from a
lot of teachers and parents,

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.900
and I've observed students as well,

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.800
that feeling of being overwhelmed,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.130
a lot is hitting everyone all at once,

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.560
and now that we're thinking about this

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.420
as being more of a longterm situation.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.630
I think one thing that I
would share right now is that,

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.450
the focus immediately had been,

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:15.350
how do I use technology with my students?

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.330
But really to take a step back and think

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:20.810
about taking those classroom norms

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:22.840
to use in the brick and mortar classroom,

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.690
and how you can transition that,

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.750
and use your students
as part of a community

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:28.210
building process of,

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:30.870
what are the norms we want for our class?

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.530
What are the norms we're going to use

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:35.020
when we use a video conferencing tool,

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.270
like zoom or Google Hangouts?

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.703
What are the things that we're gonna set

00:05:38.703 --> 00:05:41.610
into the classroom norms for
communicating with each other.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.080
So focusing on building that community

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.950
with your students and setting norms and

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:48.420
opportunities for communication first,

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.440
and then using those
tools, like Khan Academy,

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.440
to support those norms and best practices,

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:57.173
as opposed to putting
just the technology first.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.760
- And Jeremy, taking the other
side on the parents side,

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:02.980
what advice would you have for parents?

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.900
I think many of us are in this boat.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:06.620
I felt it over the last couple of weeks,

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.270
so you're feeling overwhelmed,

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.610
your kids are at home, you're
trying to work from home.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:11.690
If you have young kids,

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:14.101
they make it difficult
for you to work from home,

00:06:14.101 --> 00:06:17.050
at the same time you have a
list of do's from their school,

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:18.883
that you're trying to take pictures of it

00:06:18.883 --> 00:06:19.790
and send it to their teacher,

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:20.860
what advice do you have for parents

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.208
who are trying to navigate that?

00:06:23.208 --> 00:06:25.380
- All three of us are very
much in the same boat.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.040
I know there are millions of other parents

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.200
out there who are asking themselves

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.610
the same question right now.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.870
My own kids are causing up a
storm over in the next room,

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:35.850
so I appreciate their indulgence.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:36.990
That being said,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.700
I would come back to this idea,

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.260
from my early days as
a teacher in training,

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:43.290
which is about getting quick wins.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:46.600
So I know that as a
parent or as an educator,

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:48.210
you want to come in and
you wanna be a master

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.520
of the situation, an awesome
parent, an incredible teacher.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:52.710
But it's tough to do when everything

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.280
is changing all around you.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.200
And so what I've been looking

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.680
to do with my own kids,

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:58.840
is find one moment in a day,

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.160
when we have a really
special moment of connection.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:03.300
Maybe it's not transcendent learning,

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.900
maybe it's not massive educational gains,

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.410
which is a moment we have
maybe a game of 20 questions,

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:10.680
or would you rather do this?

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.130
Or would you rather do that
around the dinner table?

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:15.830
Where we feel like a
family in that moment,

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:17.920
and not just the family and crisis.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:18.970
And so, I think if you can just pull out

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:20.680
that one moment a day,

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:22.960
really seize upon that
and be grateful for that,

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.280
that becomes the building
block for a routine,

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:27.460
and a family tradition
that you can celebrate

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:29.540
even beyond this crisis.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:30.970
- I really liked that.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:32.150
One thing that I've started,

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:34.560
obviously there's a certain irony coming

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.340
from me or from us,

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:37.530
but what I've really enjoyed is that,

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:40.530
I have one of those
little tablet whiteboards

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:41.860
with a whiteboard marker,

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:43.980
and I've just been writing a problem

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.900
of the day for my two youngest.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.600
My oldest who's 11, he
kinda is doing his thing

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:49.610
and he's pretty productive,

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:51.410
and, and my middle one is okay.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:52.750
But just writing that problem down

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:54.200
and we sit next to each
other on the couch,

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.237
it takes 10 minutes,
but at least I'm like,

00:07:56.237 --> 00:07:59.380
"Okay, they're doing
something kind of academic."

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:01.310
And then from there you get that win

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:02.427
and then you can start layering on,

00:08:02.427 --> 00:08:04.850
"Hey, if we can do 20
minutes of Khan Academy,

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:07.710
on the math side, if we
can just read together.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:09.080
I'm a big fan of these Bob books,

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:10.480
these early learner reading things

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:11.960
that I do with my five-year-old.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:12.820
That gets you your wins,

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:14.940
and then we've published
things like these schedules,

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:17.240
that you can layer on over time.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:18.710
And actually a to do for us,

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:20.407
I think is to maybe....

00:08:20.407 --> 00:08:21.580
"cause we, these schedules could show

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:24.070
how you can use Khan Academy
for fairly full school day,

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:27.410
including breaks and
lunch and all of that,

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:29.500
we don't provide lunches,
but we can tell you

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:31.005
when they go and they could be.

00:08:31.005 --> 00:08:33.860
But I think we also should....

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:35.240
We've been saying it
in these live streams,

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:36.470
and y'all been saying it in our webinars,

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:37.580
but I think even in our schedules,

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:38.557
we should probably say,

00:08:38.557 --> 00:08:42.627
"Hey, even if you just do this
part, you're a superhero."

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:48.360
And everyone else is gravy from there.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:49.810
And actually I've gotten feedback lately,

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.610
that I say the term "Everything is gravy."

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:52.890
And a lot of people, especially

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:54.600
from other countries are
not familiar with it.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:57.150
And so the general idea is that,

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.410
mashed potatoes by themselves are enough

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:01.780
to provide the calories you need,

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:03.680
but they taste even better with gravy,

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.150
which you don't necessarily need.

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:09.599
So anyway, let's see (laughs)

00:09:09.599 --> 00:09:14.599
We have questions coming in
from, also see, so from...

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:17.830
There's couple of questions
we could all take stabs at.

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:22.360
Some of these, from
YouTube, (murmurs) asks,

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:25.400
how do you help a kid that
struggles with testing?

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:27.330
You either don't want to take that,

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:29.530
I'm happy to jump into
you Meaghan, one take

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:32.857
- Sure, I'll take a first stab at that,

00:09:32.857 --> 00:09:34.880
and I'm looking forward Sal and Jeremy

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:37.100
to your thoughts as well.

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.960
So I think for some
students it's important

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:42.749
to identify what it is about the testing

00:09:42.749 --> 00:09:44.820
that might feel daunting to them,

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:49.280
is it the content timebox element,

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:53.060
is it just the concept
of, that this individual,

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:55.420
there's one thing I'm doing
and there's moment holds

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:57.810
so much weight that that's
overwhelming for them.

00:09:57.810 --> 00:09:59.187
So I think the first step is,

00:09:59.187 --> 00:10:02.000
is having a conversation with your child

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.470
or student and trying to identify,

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:08.550
what it is that feels
like a struggle for them?

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.120
Because sometimes even that conversation

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:14.550
takes off that first level
of what's so intimidating.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:16.630
And then if you can get into more

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:19.310
of the details of, is it the content?

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:20.520
Is it the time piece?

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:24.410
Is it the concept of an
assessment that's so troublesome?

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:25.540
Once you know what that is,

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:27.680
then there's opportunities and resources

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:29.040
to kind of dig further into that,

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.490
and to work with your child or student

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:33.743
through that particular situation.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:38.073
- Yup, Jeremy, anything to add to that?

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:40.840
- Yeah, I would just say
this is actually a chance

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:42.620
for you to really sort of focus on

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:44.410
the most important things and learning

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:46.410
which are mastering new skills,

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:48.240
not just trying to get a test completed.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:49.140
I think that's actually a place

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:50.840
where Khan Academy shines.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:52.620
I know with my own six year old,

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:53.980
when she first started doing it,

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:55.770
she was really frustrated
because he was still in

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.387
that mental model of, "I have to

00:10:57.387 --> 00:11:00.280
"get 100% and I have to
do it in the first go."

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:01.450
When then she realized that she could

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:03.070
actually take the same assessment

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:07.060
over and over again,
depression started to wear off.

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:08.980
So she wasn't focused exclusively

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:10.760
on, "I need to, you know,

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:12.760
totally ACE this out of the Gates."

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:15.610
But instead build up
that muscle over time,

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:18.180
rep after rep, practice after practice

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:20.540
until I get to that Holy grail of mastery,

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:21.670
and that's what really matters.

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:24.268
And Khan Academy makes that possible.

00:11:24.268 --> 00:11:27.140
- (murmurs) go ahead Meaghan.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:29.030
- I was gonna say, I wonder if this is

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:29.980
where you were headed as well."

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:31.700
So I think it brings a great opportunity.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:33.050
Jeremy, what you're saying is that,

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:36.500
we have such great growth
mindset content on Khan Academy,

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:38.990
and I think sometimes
that concept is so new

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:42.600
to students and so different
from what they're used to,

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:44.880
in some traditional classroom settings,

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.160
that it's a great
opportunity to even leverage

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:50.530
some of the gross mindset
content that we have from....

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:51.790
That we developed in collaboration

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:53.720
with Perth's at Stanford.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:55.420
It's a great opportunity to use some

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.100
of that and work with your student

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:58.943
on building that growth mindset.

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:01.083
- And y'all are both
talking about a two....

00:12:01.083 --> 00:12:03.963
Sometimes subtle things, but things

00:12:03.963 --> 00:12:07.420
that we take very seriously
here at Khan Academy,

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:09.410
one is this notion of mastery learning,

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:11.440
which is one of the reasons

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:12.960
why there's test taking anxiety is,

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.230
you take a test, you got a 70 on it,

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.420
you're labeled a C or a D student

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:19.270
and that doesn't feel good,

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:21.250
while, all of us at Khan Academy,

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:22.360
we strongly believe in this,

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.080
is what mastery learning is all about,

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:25.267
is that you got, you took a test,

00:12:25.267 --> 00:12:26.978
you get a 70% on it, not a big deal.

00:12:26.978 --> 00:12:28.530
What Jeremy was referring to,

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:31.283
is you can then take a
very similar test again.

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:34.290
And I'm often sensitive when people try

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:36.590
to compare Khan Academy
to other resources,

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:37.760
and I look at some of these resources

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:39.460
and they'll just list like five questions.

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:40.957
And I'm like, "No, no,
no, we don't realize

00:12:40.957 --> 00:12:43.110
"we have thousands of questions.

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:45.430
So that if someone doesn't
do get it the first time,

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:49.030
they can keep taking it
without seeing a repetition

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:52.563
of the same test or the same exercises.

00:12:52.563 --> 00:12:54.070
And obviously that that takes
a pile of resources for us,

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:55.346
but we do it because
we so strongly believe

00:12:55.346 --> 00:12:59.330
that, that first shot
shouldn't be your only shot

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:00.690
and that you should keep working

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:03.040
until you can get to 70%, 80%, 90%.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:04.800
Maybe sometimes you can
move ahead if you need to,

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:07.270
but then you can go back
and remediate those gaps.

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:09.033
And what Meaghan is referring
to a growth mindset,

00:13:09.033 --> 00:13:11.110
which is very popular.

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.080
It's very involved
these days in education,

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.197
which is, you'd have a
fixed mindset where you say,

00:13:15.197 --> 00:13:16.530
"I'm either good at this or I'm not"

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:18.247
Or a growth mindset where you say,

00:13:18.247 --> 00:13:20.897
"I only know how good I
am by applying myself,

00:13:20.897 --> 00:13:23.672
"pushing myself out of my comfort zone,

00:13:23.672 --> 00:13:24.505
"being willing to fail,

00:13:24.505 --> 00:13:26.667
"recognizing that failure
is not a bad thing,

00:13:26.667 --> 00:13:28.247
"that, that's actually
when I grow the most,

00:13:28.247 --> 00:13:29.980
"if I reflect on that failure."

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.730
But a world where you
just give someone a test

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:35.010
and they get a 70 and
then that's the end of it,

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:36.720
you can't apply a growth mindset there.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:39.550
Because then that's saying
you are a 70% student,

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:42.348
in order to actually
have a growth mindset,

00:13:42.348 --> 00:13:43.510
you have to actually really
have mastery learning

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:44.670
where you get back up.

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:47.180
And you say, "Okay, I'm
going to try this again."

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:48.240
That's how it works in other things.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:50.240
If you're learning piano
and you mess up the piece,

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:52.580
people say, "Okay, you're
70% go on to the next piece."

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:54.250
No, they say, "Keep working on it"

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:57.120
Or in a martial art or if
you're shooting free throws,

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:57.977
the coach doesn't say,

00:13:57.977 --> 00:13:59.840
"Hey, you're a bad free throw shooter,

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:00.800
let's move on to dribbling."

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:04.970
They say, "No, keep
practicing those free throws."

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:06.130
So I think that's huge.

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:08.430
And Meaghan I like the
way that broke it down.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:10.220
There's a lot of things
that could be happening

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:12.310
for a student who's having
trouble with testing.

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.740
I'll throw out another
kind of metal level thing,

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:17.163
which is just meditation,

00:14:18.383 --> 00:14:20.330
which sounds fancy and sounds all new age,

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:23.540
but it's literally just sitting down

00:14:24.465 --> 00:14:25.298
and trying to quiet your thoughts,

00:14:25.298 --> 00:14:26.918
and realizing that
you're in your thoughts.

00:14:26.918 --> 00:14:29.750
I think even for very young kids,

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:30.890
that can oftentimes work

00:14:30.890 --> 00:14:32.310
'cause we can get so overwhelmed.

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:34.030
Because I think, especially when people

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:35.650
say they're bad test takers,

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:37.200
that's usually, that category

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:38.310
that you talked about, Meaghan

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:41.694
which is this notion of a test

00:14:41.694 --> 00:14:45.757
that their brain starts
having circuits go that like,

00:14:45.757 --> 00:14:46.667
"Oh my God, Oh my God,

00:14:46.667 --> 00:14:47.727
"what if I can't answer this question?

00:14:47.727 --> 00:14:49.040
"What if I can answer this question?"

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:51.410
And then it becomes a
self fulfilling prophecy.

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:53.110
I think if you could
kind of step yourself,

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:56.200
step out of those cycling of thoughts,

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:58.680
which sitting quietly everyday

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.280
for two minutes, three
minutes, five minutes,

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:02.140
20 minutes can really help.

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:04.060
So there's a couple of questions here.

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:06.010
This is a popular topic.

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:09.750
One is YouTube Leon and YouTube 60106

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:10.960
I'll ask both of them,

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:12.920
and please discuss the transition

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:14.100
from missions to masteries,

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:15.670
world of math was unique
and showing a more

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:17.160
unified view of the field,

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:19.070
can we create a comparable
view in the new system?

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:21.780
And then 60106 asks,

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:22.727
'Hi Sal, why have you chosen

00:15:22.727 --> 00:15:24.487
"to remove missions from Khan Academy?

00:15:24.487 --> 00:15:26.090
"Will you replace them
with something else?"

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:29.320
Well, I'll take a first step, which is,

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:31.610
for those of you who don't know (mumbles)

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:33.770
it's the part of Khan
Academy historically,

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:34.603
and it's still there today.

00:15:34.603 --> 00:15:35.840
Although people are asking questions

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:37.667
because we are what's known
in the software field,

00:15:37.667 --> 00:15:39.520
you know, we're deprecating it,

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:41.730
which means over time
it's going to go away.

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:44.700
Missions were a way to get recommendations

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:45.550
on what to do next.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:46.840
Kids get practice feedback,

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:48.440
there's all this game mechanics,

00:15:48.440 --> 00:15:50.010
there are things called mastery challenges

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:51.350
to make sure that you've mastered

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:53.200
the concepts and level up your mastery.

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:56.703
And the reason why we
are deprecating missions,

00:15:57.744 --> 00:15:59.370
is there's another
experience on Khan Academy,

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:01.850
which internally we've
called our library view.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:04.130
This is when a lot of y'all
have done web searches,

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:06.047
You fell on Khan Academy
and then just like,

00:16:06.047 --> 00:16:09.080
"Oh, okay there's a video there,
there's an exercise there."

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:10.340
But what we want to do is,

00:16:10.340 --> 00:16:11.430
Khan Academy mission should feel like,

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:12.540
what a good tutor would do.

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:14.310
It will help you, if you're cramming

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:15.510
for that exam tomorrow

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:16.420
or you're trying to figure out

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:19.070
how to factor quadratics
for your homework tonight,

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:20.497
but what a good tutor would do is,

00:16:20.497 --> 00:16:22.667
"Hey, we're there to help you right now,

00:16:22.667 --> 00:16:24.697
"but you and I should keep working

00:16:24.697 --> 00:16:26.317
"together 20 minutes a day,

00:16:26.317 --> 00:16:29.317
"so that you don't get into
this stressful situation again."

00:16:30.458 --> 00:16:32.340
And historically, missions
were where that happens,

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:33.900
but it was kind of a
different part of the site

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:35.550
and you have to click around to find it.

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:36.947
And a couple of years ago we said,

00:16:36.947 --> 00:16:40.660
"Well, what if we could merge
the best of our library view

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:41.750
and our missions view?

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:42.910
And that's what we are...

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:45.710
Internally, we call course
mastery on Khan Academy.

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:48.010
But now when you go to what used

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:49.700
to be the library on Khan Academy,

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:51.700
you'll see that it'll talk about mastery

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:53.610
and mastery points and it gives you

00:16:54.541 --> 00:16:56.030
these little visuals that
talk about your mastery level,

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:57.210
and you can uplevel them

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:58.810
by working on a particular skill,

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:01.840
working on quizzes, unit
tests, mastery challenges.

00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:02.890
We just introduced a couple

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:04.860
of months ago, course challenges.

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:06.750
And so we are trying to bring everything

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:09.920
that missions are into the course mastery.

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:12.040
And it's already getting quite close.

00:17:12.040 --> 00:17:14.820
And so the plan is,
before we fully deprecate

00:17:15.655 --> 00:17:17.480
or sunset admissions,

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:19.830
we will have our course mastery,

00:17:19.830 --> 00:17:21.060
do everything that missions do,

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:23.150
and all the things that
the library does well,

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:24.830
which gives you more contacts,

00:17:24.830 --> 00:17:26.680
it makes it easier for you to jump around,

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:29.670
understand why certain things
are connected to others.

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:32.070
When Leon asks about the world of math,

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.630
that was a mission where you essentially

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:35.883
to try and do all of math on Khan Academy.

00:17:37.122 --> 00:17:37.955
And I love that notion.

00:17:37.955 --> 00:17:39.967
I was obviously one of
the first people said,

00:17:39.967 --> 00:17:40.817
"Hey, there should just be a place

00:17:40.817 --> 00:17:42.510
"where you learn everything,

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:43.500
because it is all connected."

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:44.720
And so, we could...

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:46.120
I'm not sure if we have a course,

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:47.750
but it would be nice to create a course

00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:49.590
and of course master that's world of math

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:51.323
and that should get you
where you need to get go.

00:17:51.323 --> 00:17:55.117
So from YouTube, Susanna
Garcia Domingez asks,

00:17:55.117 --> 00:17:56.097
"When school starts again,

00:17:56.097 --> 00:17:57.627
"is it going to be business as usual

00:17:57.627 --> 00:17:59.017
"or will we see changes?

00:17:59.017 --> 00:18:01.570
"What the changes do
you see taking shape?"

00:18:01.570 --> 00:18:03.770
I love to hear your
thoughts first, Meaghan.

00:18:05.170 --> 00:18:07.400
- I think that there's a
couple of things that...

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:09.560
Again, this is all evolving, right?

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:11.680
This is a whole new world for all of us.

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:14.683
And I think some things
that we are expecting,

00:18:16.357 --> 00:18:18.510
we see some are side
usually with students,

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:20.740
and sound, Jeremy, please feel
free to jump in here. Right?

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:22.750
We see some are side for students.

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:24.120
Now we're looking at a world

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:27.260
where students have had
even longer than that in

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:30.570
the United States especially
to look at that side

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.000
and try and fill in those
gaps from the year before.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:33.922
And I think Khan Academy
has some opportunities

00:18:33.922 --> 00:18:38.922
for teachers to leverage some of our tools

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.320
to be able to identify some of those gaps

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:43.460
for students to try and
get them back up to speed.

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:45.510
But we've seen this longer gap,

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:48.030
between when students left
the classroom this spring

00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:51.050
and when they hopefully are
back in the classroom this fall.

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:52.890
The other thing is we are seeing

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:54.493
that there is some possibility

00:18:54.493 --> 00:18:57.370
that the school year for 2021,

00:18:57.370 --> 00:18:58.650
could be intermittent

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:00.810
with times in and out of the classroom.

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:02.837
And so I know there's a lot of people

00:19:02.837 --> 00:19:05.100
all over the place
trying to work on pieces

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:06.560
to best solve this problem.

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:08.600
And I know that we at Khan Academy,

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:11.441
are thinking about additional ways

00:19:11.441 --> 00:19:12.360
and additional resources to

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:15.290
support parents, teachers, and students,

00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:18.160
with remote learning and teaching,

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:19.440
right now of course,

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:22.690
but also, how can we continue
that to make that transition,

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:24.360
especially if it's intermittent?

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:26.640
How can you try and
make that more seamless

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.653
and feel a little more
comfortable for everyone?

00:19:30.893 --> 00:19:31.726
- Yup, Jeremy, anything to add to that?

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:35.180
- Yeah, so I think in addition
to sort of the challenges

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:38.290
that this crisis has dealt
on entire education system.

00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:40.930
There is at least one silver
lining that I have identified,

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:42.100
which is so many of the teachers

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:43.787
who have been coming to
our webinars are saying,

00:19:43.787 --> 00:19:45.773
"For the first time in my whole career,

00:19:46.608 --> 00:19:48.210
"I'm finally getting
this technology stuff."

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:49.660
And as someone who never talked about

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:52.360
technology once in my entire
teacher training program,

00:19:53.913 --> 00:19:55.386
I know that it can often feel like this

00:19:55.386 --> 00:19:56.580
foreign world that we're entering into,

00:19:56.580 --> 00:19:58.410
but now that we're
getting these new skills,

00:19:58.410 --> 00:20:00.650
just like our students are
always learning new things,

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:02.200
we can bring new
experiences to our students.

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:04.440
And so I'm hopeful that,

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:06.487
whenever school resumes business as usual,

00:20:06.487 --> 00:20:08.049
it's going to be business
as usual, but better,

00:20:08.049 --> 00:20:10.876
with new technology and
new tools, new resources

00:20:10.876 --> 00:20:13.204
to serve students even more effectively.

00:20:13.204 --> 00:20:16.030
- Yeah, and I'll just add to that,

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:17.840
you know, everything
we've been talking about,

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:19.280
the value of personalized learning ,

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:21.560
where you're a teacher with
a classroom of 30 students

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:22.930
who are all at different levels,

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:24.600
all have different gaps,

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:26.550
You wanna do mastery learning,

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:29.180
but without the aid of technology,

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:30.810
it's very hard to do mastery learning.

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:32.410
You'd have to have multiple tests

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:33.952
that you're administered

00:20:33.952 --> 00:20:35.173
to teach the students at different time,

00:20:36.464 --> 00:20:38.340
and somehow try to orchestrate
kids on their own path.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:39.270
But the reason...

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:40.810
But the need for those things,

00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:42.490
is even going to be stronger to the point

00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:44.060
that Meaghan just made of,

00:20:44.060 --> 00:20:47.180
kids are gonna be out of school
for five months, six months.

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:49.650
Our partners at the
NWA, the not for profit

00:20:49.650 --> 00:20:51.100
that administers the map growth,

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.270
they just released a study last week

00:20:53.270 --> 00:20:55.080
talking about how those five months.

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:57.190
It's not just gonna be five
months of lost learning,

00:20:57.190 --> 00:20:59.040
it's actually gonna be
five months of forgetting.

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:01.330
So it's very possible
that the median student,

00:21:01.330 --> 00:21:03.180
if they don't get any
form of intervention,

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:05.300
they don't use Khan Academy,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:07.930
they might enter next year exactly

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:09.150
where they were a year ago.

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:11.230
So you would have a year of loss.

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:12.720
And so it's super important

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:13.920
that we leverage this time,

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:16.020
not just the remaining
time in the school year,

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:18.437
but the summer to keep learning.

00:21:18.437 --> 00:21:20.830
And then when we go back to school,

00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:23.420
the reality is, some kids
would have kept learning

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:25.370
and other kids for many reasons,

00:21:25.370 --> 00:21:26.620
might not have kept learning.

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:29.000
So they're gonna have
even a wider variation.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:32.060
And so I think it's going
to be even more imperative.

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:33.080
And it's what Meaghan and
Jeremy were talking about,

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:34.890
that when people come back,

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:36.380
we hope that they can leverage us

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:39.430
to understand where their kids are

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:42.860
and then allow more time than normal.

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:44.390
If I were to theorize,

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:46.310
I think we have talked to some districts

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:48.318
that are thinking about starting the

00:21:48.318 --> 00:21:49.151
school year a little bit early,

00:21:49.151 --> 00:21:50.770
I think especially in something like math,

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:52.632
although I think reading as well,

00:21:52.632 --> 00:21:55.455
but especially math where,
once you have those gaps,

00:21:55.455 --> 00:21:56.288
it can be very debilitating

00:21:56.288 --> 00:21:57.850
to build on top of them.

00:21:57.850 --> 00:21:59.400
It might make sense for everybody

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:03.110
to have a double block or
something for a month or two,

00:22:03.110 --> 00:22:05.070
so they can really remediate and catch up.

00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:07.290
And if you do have that type of time,

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:08.700
that's where our resources,

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:10.340
like Khan Academy could
be really valuable.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:13.763
Like, I could imagine
a month of really deep,

00:22:13.763 --> 00:22:16.040
everyone starts at...

00:22:16.040 --> 00:22:18.230
We had Tim Vanderburgh and I know

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:20.300
Jeremy did a webinar with him as well,

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:22.120
amazing teacher in Hisperia in California

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:24.190
who has all of his sixth graders start

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:25.280
at kindergarten level,

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:26.600
and Khan Academy, if they know it,

00:22:26.600 --> 00:22:27.710
they're able to accelerate ahead.

00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:30.100
If they don't, well they need
that to fill in those gaps.

00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:33.311
Within a week or two,
they're all operating,

00:22:33.311 --> 00:22:35.710
closer and closer to their grade level.

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:37.859
And I think there's concepts like that,

00:22:37.859 --> 00:22:41.910
that we could do throughout
the country or the world

00:22:41.910 --> 00:22:43.630
to help make sure that
everyone has their gaps.

00:22:43.630 --> 00:22:44.940
And if there's another silver lining

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:46.420
to what Jeremy at it is,

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:47.530
I think this is the first time

00:22:47.530 --> 00:22:49.390
that people are gonna
take summer seriously,

00:22:49.390 --> 00:22:50.680
as a time to learn.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:53.380
And I think people are going to realize

00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:56.000
that the variation is so
large to this back to school,

00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:00.765
that I think more school
districts and schools will say,

00:23:00.765 --> 00:23:02.880
"Okay, we've got to take up
this whatever this month,

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:04.930
to reduce this variance."

00:23:04.930 --> 00:23:06.090
So let everyone fill in their gaps.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:07.270
And that was always an issue,

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:08.380
but the issue is so big now

00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:10.390
that you can't ignore it.

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:12.569
So let's see.

00:23:12.569 --> 00:23:15.225
There's a whole class of questions here.

00:23:15.225 --> 00:23:17.590
YouTube Nikila go vendor asks,

00:23:17.590 --> 00:23:19.775
are there suggestions
you have for teachers

00:23:19.775 --> 00:23:21.650
who are teaching children
remotely without wifi?

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:23.570
Then boy TJR asks,

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:24.403
the app is amazing,

00:23:24.403 --> 00:23:26.960
but if you don't have good
wifi or no wifi at all.

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:28.740
And then Zachary Meadows is asking,

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.450
how could we help kids without internet?

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:31.580
I'd love your all's thoughts.

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:35.743
I'm once again happy to
give my 2 cents, Meaghan?

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:40.048
- Sure, from a Khan Academy standpoint,

00:23:40.048 --> 00:23:43.076
a lot of our system is highly
dependent on consistent

00:23:43.076 --> 00:23:45.410
internet access and device access,

00:23:45.410 --> 00:23:47.180
which we know is a challenge

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:49.260
for many students all over the globe.

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:51.480
And so, there is one thing

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:53.940
that I can recommend here is that,

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:56.460
our friends at Learning Equality,

00:23:56.460 --> 00:23:59.330
have developed a system where,

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:00.940
if you have internet access,

00:24:00.940 --> 00:24:02.390
even for a small amount of time,

00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:05.320
you can download some of
the aspects of Khan Academy

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:06.600
and use those offline.

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:07.930
So if your students have access,

00:24:07.930 --> 00:24:09.880
even to a limited amount of internet,

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:12.650
even for a small window
during the day or in the week,

00:24:12.650 --> 00:24:14.990
you can leverage that integration so

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:16.360
that students can still have access

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:18.110
to something like Khan Academy.

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:21.070
And I would say, even if they
don't have internet access,

00:24:21.070 --> 00:24:22.800
one of the biggest things we keep seeing

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:25.330
for teachers and students and parents,

00:24:25.330 --> 00:24:28.060
is still finding a way
to contact your students.

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:30.430
And I saw this really great post

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:31.920
from a teacher this week,

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:34.120
where they wrote handwritten notes

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:35.150
for each of their students

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:37.380
and even drop them off at their homes,

00:24:37.380 --> 00:24:39.880
which we don't even think about

00:24:39.880 --> 00:24:41.627
snail mail anymore for students.

00:24:41.627 --> 00:24:42.960
And some of them don't even think

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:44.620
about that type of communication.

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:47.280
But what a powerful impact
that had on her students

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:49.050
to know that the teacher,

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:51.060
and she was still thinking about them,

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:53.550
even if she couldn't see
them face to face every day.

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:55.690
So I would leverage finding ways

00:24:55.690 --> 00:24:57.100
to communicate with your students,

00:24:57.100 --> 00:24:59.558
even if you can't see them
directly on a video call.

00:24:59.558 --> 00:25:04.558
- Yeah, and I'll just add to that,

00:25:04.593 --> 00:25:08.430
for teachers who are
teaching kids with out wifi.

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:10.786
I've seen teachers do things like,

00:25:10.786 --> 00:25:11.853
the students do their work handwritten,

00:25:12.703 --> 00:25:14.340
but at least use a parent's
phone to text them an image,

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:16.550
which I think is reasonably common.

00:25:16.550 --> 00:25:19.470
And Khan Academy is usable
on, on a cell phone.

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:23.150
So I know there's still
people who might not have.....

00:25:23.150 --> 00:25:24.740
They might be data sensitive

00:25:26.267 --> 00:25:27.926
or not have wifi access,

00:25:27.926 --> 00:25:28.759
but as long as a family
member has a cell phone

00:25:28.759 --> 00:25:31.090
that has a data plan, a smartphone,

00:25:31.090 --> 00:25:35.240
they should be able to use
us in some way shape or form.

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:37.170
We are hearing really good stuff coming.

00:25:37.170 --> 00:25:40.360
I've talked to Dr. Jara from
Las Vegas, from Clark County.

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:42.360
They've been distributing laptops

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:44.250
for kids who don't have it.

00:25:44.250 --> 00:25:46.470
I talked to Ray Daleo
and his wife Barbara,

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:48.892
in Connecticut, they just bought laptops.

00:25:48.892 --> 00:25:50.700
They're incredible philanthropists

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:53.853
who bought laptops for, I think 40, 50,

00:25:53.853 --> 00:25:57.350
60,000 high school
students in Connecticut.

00:25:57.350 --> 00:25:59.230
So there are programs out there,

00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:00.910
and so I wouldn't be surprised,

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:03.210
Obviously I don't know
where everyone is from,

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:05.780
but call your local cable company.

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:09.243
I think they are leaning into it now,

00:26:09.243 --> 00:26:10.478
so I think they'd be inclined

00:26:10.478 --> 00:26:12.301
to give you internet access at home

00:26:12.301 --> 00:26:13.134
or open up other hotspots

00:26:13.134 --> 00:26:14.693
that might be in the same building as you,

00:26:14.693 --> 00:26:17.430
they might call your school district,

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:19.540
there might be ways to
get access to devices.

00:26:19.540 --> 00:26:20.570
I've seen everyone's trying

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:22.240
to do right by folks right now,

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:25.520
so don't hesitate to reach out.

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:26.540
I think you'll be surprised

00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:28.405
how much help you might be able to get.

00:26:28.405 --> 00:26:32.393
The other questions.

00:26:34.846 --> 00:26:36.483
So from Facebook, (murmurs) asks,

00:26:37.389 --> 00:26:38.222
do you pull kids that use kinda kind of

00:26:38.222 --> 00:26:40.215
and see what they like and don't like

00:26:40.215 --> 00:26:41.674
about the content structure, et cetera,

00:26:41.674 --> 00:26:43.040
and use info to make changes?

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:45.360
So the simple answer is, "Yes, kind of."

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:47.540
We do put our stuff in front of students,

00:26:47.540 --> 00:26:48.790
we do see how it's like,

00:26:48.790 --> 00:26:49.810
we do user research,

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:51.200
We do that with teachers as well,

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:54.840
And we get the data on what
people are finding, engaging,

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:56.930
what changes to the site
seem to work, what don't,

00:26:56.930 --> 00:26:58.810
and we run efficacy studies

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:02.173
to understand is it really
improving their outcomes?

00:27:04.779 --> 00:27:07.777
Um, so Bill Emma from YouTube says,

00:27:11.237 --> 00:27:13.267
"We are not able to keep
continuous concentration

00:27:13.267 --> 00:27:14.957
"on studies in isolation, My question is

00:27:14.957 --> 00:27:16.650
"how can we overcome the situation?"

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:19.630
Need a suggestion from
Meaghan, all right Meaghan.

00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:24.630
- Wow (murmurs), yeah, focus is hard.

00:27:24.994 --> 00:27:26.393
And I would say student motivation

00:27:26.393 --> 00:27:28.064
and even personal
motivation is hard, right?

00:27:28.064 --> 00:27:31.940
I was a classroom teacher for nine years

00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:33.950
and a big piece of my focus and

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:35.830
motivation definitely leveraged my

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:38.521
face to face time with students.

00:27:38.521 --> 00:27:39.660
And let's not neglect the fact that face

00:27:39.660 --> 00:27:41.853
to face time with colleagues, right?

00:27:41.853 --> 00:27:43.010
That's really helps you focus working

00:27:43.010 --> 00:27:45.370
as a group or motivating students.

00:27:45.370 --> 00:27:49.220
And so I think one
piece to think about is,

00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:50.580
maybe as a teacher or a parent,

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:53.730
finding opportunities to communicate

00:27:53.730 --> 00:27:55.310
with your colleagues or friends,

00:27:55.310 --> 00:27:57.560
finding ways to be still social

00:27:57.560 --> 00:27:59.800
and share best practices or shares, ideas,

00:27:59.800 --> 00:28:00.650
things that are working right.

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:02.370
This is a challenge for everyone,

00:28:02.370 --> 00:28:04.130
and while it can feel really isolating,

00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:06.430
it doesn't have to be as isolating,

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:09.110
still finding ways to work as a teacher

00:28:09.110 --> 00:28:11.980
or a peer learning
community or as a parent,

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:13.650
even finding time to share,

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:16.960
like what might or might not
be working with your children.

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:18.090
I think that's really important.

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:20.090
And when it comes to student motivation,

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:21.350
I think that's a big piece

00:28:21.350 --> 00:28:23.010
of helping them stay focused,

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:26.660
and work seeing some
really unique things coming

00:28:27.581 --> 00:28:29.630
from teachers and writing
necessity breeds innovation.

00:28:29.630 --> 00:28:32.960
And so we're seeing teachers find ways

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:35.243
of things around their house or saying,

00:28:36.230 --> 00:28:38.040
this many of you complete the assignment

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:40.144
or you make this much progress,

00:28:40.144 --> 00:28:42.450
thinking about our mastery system.

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:44.710
If you make it to master in this scale,

00:28:44.710 --> 00:28:49.210
I will walk on Legos
or grow up my mustache

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:54.080
or do a tick talk video in my
children's Halloween costume.

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:56.540
So we're finding really unique ways

00:28:56.540 --> 00:28:59.180
for teachers to still motivate students,

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:01.343
even when they're not face to face.

